Chinese words are classified by pronunciation and dictionary meaning:
'you' meaning especially/still is stronger than common 'you' meaning have.
Standalone syllables respond more than whole words. Korean yu, Japanese yu
and long vowels, and several cross-token vowel/glide spellings also respond.
Japanese context strengthens weaker Chinese you-syllables.

UTF-8 fragments are joined, with a stronger response on character completion.
The ambiguous two-byte start of 欧 already responds, even before the full
character is known. Repeated middle dots have a separate negative response.
Other positions are mostly zero. State resets at document boundaries.
These are selected responses, not a complete phonetic account of the component.
